This was my final ascension in the 2004 Tournament. It became clear I
was not going to have time to do one of each race after my dismally
stupid death with an elven priestess, so I decided it would be best to
try a neutral character and do one of each alignment. I picked
randomly from the available options; a gnomish archaeologist was easy
meat for an ettin mummy after narrowly managing to polish off a horde
of ants at Mine Town, but the very next character I was handed was the
same thing I ascended first; a neutral human valkyrie.

She began with Str 18/04, which made it easy to gamble on getting
poison resistance; I got it from the very first corpse, reducing that
to 18/01. Another stroke of luck was that the Mine Town temple was
coaligned, but as it turned out I made relatively little use of it.
Loki provided 3 crossaligned temples on 13, 15 and 25 - the latter
extremely handy as Ludious was also on 25. Izchak had a magic lamp,
which saved me the usual business of collecting brass lanterns and oil
lamps - fortunate, since there were relatively few to be had.

My pet vanished, but a giant ant deathdropped a figurine of a giant
ant, which turned out tame - this let me rob the Mine Town shops
easily. There wasn't much else that was unusual in the opening game;
an early altar gave me Mjollnir (and later Vorpy along with some junk
artifacts); Mines first (with a hezrou on Mines 6 - but not a bones
file, oddly enough) with the annoying Catacombs ending.

On the way down to Sokoban I found a huge general store which I robbed
perfectly well (dug a hole outside the door, moved onto it until I
escaped it (which once dropped me down a bunch of levels, which was
irritating), teleported out) except that I forgot to pick up the Fire
Brand before doing so. Oops. So I had to go back in, poly the
shopkeeper into an uruk-hai, and surround him with pits to avoid
accidentally killing him. Fire Brand and Mojo did me for the rest of
the game; I brought Vorpy to Gehennom and the Endgame, but never used
it.

I used the idea posted on rgrn recently of blocking off all but the
first chamber of Sokoban after completing it, so that I could do stash
operations with impunity. My primary stash lived here for the rest of
the game; now I've realised the benefits of controlled levelport, this
was not a nuisance. The only irritation was that I kept it all in what
proved to the only sack I found besides the Sokoban BoH (personally, I
prefer the AoR Sokoban. The BoH saves a lot of tedious shuffling, but
the AoR is more likely to save your life); then before the final
ascent I had to dump all the junk out of it to use it as an emergency
stuff sack.

A WoCancel saved me from doing tedious dilution stuff, and also worked
quite well in conjunction with the first real win; smoky potions were
fruit juice. This is about as good as it gets; it's easy to turn
useless potions into it by unihorning sickness, amethysting booze
(took me 30 seconds to find an amethyst on the Mine End level), and
cancelling see invisible; both it and its constituents are common; and
unlike full healing (which you will also want to make a lot of) you
don't need to save them to actually use (and then possibly get screwed
by getting the wish when you needed the HP).

I found an AoESP early (which I wore for the rest of the game); got
GDSM quite early from a throne wish, was crowned shortly post-Sokoban,
and then got a wish from the very first smoky potion I tried which
netted me a SoR. After that I felt I'd have to pull a pretty
spectacular bonehead maneuver to fail... but after the priestess, I
wasn't taking any chances. Since I had no GoP (irritatingly, I didn't
get them until the Castle wand) I lugged my tinning kit down to the
Quest home level to eat giants even though I was only XL 12; however,
while I was doing so offing the giants got me to 13 and a PoGL got me
to 14, so I did the Quest.

My only worry was the drawbridges (and general care around the lava)
so I brought a WoStriking and jumping boots. Surtur kindly came out to
meet me, but after I'd breathed on him gently (and he died) I wanted
to loot his inner chamber, so they came in handy anyway.

After this, I picked up the game again with only a few hours left to
run in the tournament; few enough that I was unsure about the wisdom
of clearing out Ludious. In the end I went for it, but breached my
usual rule about conflict simply to save clock time - if blinded, you
don't have a vast number of messages to grind through, and the
reduction in game turns then reduces clock time as well. I didn't get
much out of it besides a one-point reduction in naked AC to AC 0 and
the usual piles of K-rats and items, but it didn't hurt.

Cleaned out the Castle (through the back door as always - I had no
choice this time, though, since I'd forgotten to bring a
bridge-nuker), continuing to use conflict to speed things along.  I
found speed boots in the armoury, saving a wish, and the wand was
0:2. However, I'd also found plenty of charging scrolls - rare as that
is - and got a second smoky wish in pre-Gehennom preparation at my
stash. I compensated for this by wasting 2 wishes on a robe and a helm
of brilliance in order to try magic mapping; but with the failure rate
still at 80% and very little mana, this was a pointless exercise. I
should instead have used a helm of telepathy to free up the amulet
slot for lifesaving. I genocided h and L at this juncture, and R later.

Someone on #nethack suggested the trick of levelporting to the VS with
one of the major demons - I used Asmodeus. I still had to magic-map
via scrolls or my minimal casting chance in the Vlad's range, but that
was quite feasible; and with the VS on 46, Gehennom was quite short.
This operation was all quite routine really, and I returned to my
stash to prepare for the final run - where a last fruit juice session
netted me 2 more wishes, one with the very last potion. Some games you
just get lucky.

I conflicted through the Tower and the Sanctum; I'd had no cPoGL, but
this let me escape quickly. I had relatively little trouble with the
mysterious force, except around Orcus, but still saw plenty of Rodney;
however, since I had two WoDeath and the charging scrolls to use them,
and this meant fewer malignant auras, that was not a problem. The
usual drop into the stash on the way back (and the endless sack-
shuffling operation mentioned earlier); no real trouble on the Planes
apart from 2 Archons on Fire, but I blindfolded myself and polished
off the first one, and the second was too far behind to block me from
the portal.

I did not use conflict on the Astral Plane. I did Death first as
usual, and maneuvered around her on the way in without getting
attacked; had to kill her on the way out, but MoJo works well for
action at a distance. I used one of my confused taming shots on that
first altar. Famine was relatively uncrowded; took some attacks from
him, but no serious worries. Once again, however, this was a third
altar job; but I had enough zap left in the WoDeath to nuke Pestilence
and struggle through to the final altar with around half an hour to
spare.

